youranmod.jei.arf_laser_cavity.info=Made on the Light Source blueprint at the Engineer's Workbench; it comes out empty. Fill liquid argon with an IC2 fluid canner or an IE bottling machine, 10 mB at a time, up to 750 mB; add fluorine and neon with an IC2 solid canner, one Alchemistry element at a time. How you mix the three decides the laser output (the "expected output" in the tooltip) - find out yourself. Craft it with an IE floodlight into an excimer laser light source; put the light source alone in a crafting grid to take the cavity back out and refill it.
youranmod.jei.hene_laser_tube.info=Made on the Light Source blueprint (glass or quartz tube); it comes out empty. Add helium and neon with an IC2 solid canner, one Alchemistry element at a time, up to 250 in a tube; the mix is up to you. Put it in the aligner's laser slot together with a personal computer motherboard running the laser alignment control program to use laser alignment.
youranmod.jei.excimer_light_source.info=Crafted from an ArF laser cavity and an IE floodlight. Goes in the light source spot of the lithography machine instead of a mercury light source: 0.05 μm resolution, 1024 FE/t while exposing, and every exposure uses up a little fluorine.
youranmod.jei.improved_resist.info=1000 mB positive resist + 10 mB IC2 UU-matter → 1000 mB improved positive resist: IE refinery, or an IC2 canner (enrich mode: positive resist in the input tank, one container holding UU-matter in the input slot; each run draws 10 mB from it). 0.05 μm resolution; coating, development and spoiling work as for positive resist.
ie.manual.entry.lithography5=Excimer laser light source (DUV): craft an ArF laser cavity with a floodlight. 0.05 μm resolution (mercury lamps reach 1.5 μm at best), 1024 FE/t while exposing. The cavity is made on the Light Source blueprint; you fill the argon, fluorine and neon yourself and the mix decides the output. Every exposure uses a little fluorine and the output drops with it; take it out and add fluorine. YR-2000 needs it together with improved positive resist.
ie.manual.entry.lithography6=Laser alignment: put a personal computer motherboard (ROM: the laser alignment control program, as a mask ROM or an EPROM holding the genuine image) and a He-Ne laser tube in the two top-right slots of the main screen. Coarse-align with the knobs until the offset is inside the capture range (about ±1 μm), then press Laser align: within seconds the residual comes down to about 0.01 μm (uses power), and drift after each chip is corrected automatically.
ie.manual.entry.lithography7=Drifting out of the capture range, turning a knob or taking the structure apart means coarse-aligning again. A faster CPU converges faster; a weaker laser converges slower and less precisely. The alignment light must be a wavelength the resist ignores: an ArF cavity or excimer light source in the laser slot still aligns, but it exposes the coated resist and that chip is scrap.
