desc.immersiveengineering.info.blueprint.light_source=Light Source
ie.manual.entry.lithography.name=Lithography Machine
ie.manual.entry.lithography.subtext=Printing masks onto wafers
ie.manual.entry.lithography0=Lithography Machine: 7 wide, 4 deep, 4 tall. Build it as shown (a light source, mercury or excimer laser, in the middle of layer 2) and hit the alignment table with an Engineer's Hammer.
ie.manual.entry.lithography1=Pipes and power go in the back: on layer 0, from left, steam/distilled water out, developer in, etchant in; on layer 1, steam in, power (light engineering block), resist in. Hopper on the left takes wafers, dopants and casings; dropper on the right outputs chips. The redstone block at the front-left corner stops the machine; hammer it to invert.
ie.manual.entry.lithography2=Step into the yellow booth and use the alignment table. Masks go in by hand only. Each layer: oxidation, coating, align & expose, development, etching; layer count depends on the mask. Then doping, metallization, dicing and packaging. The wafer is consumed only when the chip comes out.
ie.manual.entry.lithography3=Alignment: bring both wafer crosses into the mask windows. Translation moves both views together, rotation moves them oppositely; knobs have backlash. Each chip drifts a little; below 50% realign. Disassembling (to change the lamp) also requires realignment and loses tank contents, so drain the tanks first.
ie.manual.entry.lithography4=The alignment table is the hammer trigger. A light source (mercury or excimer laser) keeps its own data and stays in place when forming or disassembling; changing it means taking the structure apart.
